{
  "format": "kingmaker",
  "version": 2,
  "kind": "scene",
  "id": "toll-bridge",
  "title": "Garrow's Toll Bridge",
  "description": "The one patrol on the King's Road pinned behind a hay wain, a toll-warden who won't open his door, and a goblin war-boss on a worg across the river.",
  "lore": [
    "World/King's Road.md"
  ],
  "time": "evening",
  "place": "toll-bridge",
  "hook": "A mile on from the crossroads, the King's Road crosses the river by Garrow's toll bridge, and that's where the smoke is coming from. Somewhere behind you Fitch is getting his cart moving. Ahead: shouting, a drum, a horn, and goblins.",
  "setting": "Dusk, two days before the Centennial Assembly, at Garrow's toll bridge: a humpbacked stone bridge over a river in winter spate (brown, loud and freezing, its parapet long since gone into it), the only crossing for miles. At the near end Garrow's whitewashed toll house, shuttered and barred, smoke curling from its chimney; the red-and-white toll pole across the road burning like a torch where the goblins lit it. Across the road at the bridge foot the patrol has dragged over a hay wain for cover, its hay spilled, goblin arrows in its boards, and behind it two palace guards in steel caps and the King's green, one of them hurt. Goblins round the toll-house corner with their backs to the road, a drum and a bow; and over the river, out of sight beyond the willows, a horn and something that howls. Beyond the bridge the road runs on east to Caerwyn, five miles, the city's lamps not yet lit.",
  "premise": "Can the player break the goblins' hold on the bridge and save the King's patrol, and what will they make of the war-boss when he comes?",
  "truth": "The war-boss Grisk has brought the burnt mill's war-band down to Garrow's bridge for the biggest show yet before the Assembly, as the longlegs pays them to: be seen, burn the toll pole, frighten the city. He didn't expect a patrol, and when Corporal Haskett and Guardsman Wren rode out to meet the King's cushions his archers shot their horses from under them; they've been pinned behind the hay wain since. Wartle and two of the band (the rearguard) hold the near bank; Grisk himself waits across the river with his worg and a spearman, to cross once the soldiers break. Garrow has been barricaded in his toll house since the first arrow, writing it all down, as he writes down everything: his ledger has twelve palace grain wagons crossing inward this month, laden, which the city says were lost to goblins. Grisk is a goblin boss, not a fool: he fights from his worg's back while it's winning and quits when it isn't, and he'd sell his own band for his life, along with what he knows of a lady rider Chief Gubbin took at a ford a week ago for the longlegs. Nobody here knows who the longlegs is, and it isn't this scene's to explain: the ledger and the pennies are puzzles to carry on.",
  "clock": "The rearguard's fight comes almost at once: as soon as the player shows themselves or does anything, or with the first exchange at the latest. After it, a short breath behind the hay wain (the guards, Garrow through his shutters, the horn across the river), and then the war-boss comes, felt before he's seen (the horn across the river, a howl, the drum falling silent): within three exchanges of the rearguard's fight, sooner if the player crosses the bridge or calls him out. After that fight Fitch's cart comes up the road, and the light going is felt a step at a time and never the same sign twice: Wren wanting to get the player to the King before the gates shut at the ninth bell, a step more urgently each exchange.",
  "limits": [
    "Crossing the river anywhere but the bridge: it's in winter spate, deep and fast enough to drown a horse, and the nearest ford is miles upstream.",
    "More help from the city: the gates are five miles off and every guard is lining the Assembly route; nobody is coming but whoever is on the road already.",
    "Getting into the toll house: Garrow has barred his door and shutters and won't open them to anyone while there's a goblin in sight (after, he opens to the King's green, grudgingly).",
    "Healing beyond what the player and the rules can do: Haskett has a field dressing and nothing else, and the toll house has a stove, a kettle and a bottle of brandy.",
    "Chasing the goblins to the burnt mill: it's half a day north past the woods, and the light's gone.",
    "Knowing who the longlegs is, or what the ledger's wagons mean: nobody here does. They're puzzles to carry on into the city."
  ],
  "known": [
    "There are no patrols on the King's Road this week but this one: every guard is lining the Assembly route.",
    "Garrow takes the King's penny, a silver penny a head, and writes everyone in his book.",
    "The city gates shut at the ninth bell this week, for the Assembly; the King's green goes through any gate.",
    "The King takes his guests' comfort very seriously, down to the cushions."
  ],
  "cast": [
    {
      "id": "haskett",
      "name": "Corporal Maud Haskett",
      "called": "the corporal with the halberd",
      "aka": [
        "Haskett",
        "the corporal"
      ],
      "lore": "Cast/Corporal Maud Haskett/public.md",
      "look": "A square, weathered woman of fifty in a dented steel cap and the palace guard's green, with a halberd taller than she is and a goblin arrow through the meat of her left shoulder that she has snapped off short and is ignoring. A face like a slammed door, and kind eyes she keeps for emergencies.",
      "voice": "Barks, in a sergeant's shorthand ('Down. Now. Good.'). Swears inventively and never the same way twice. Calls Wren 'lad' and the player 'you' until they've earned better, then 'friend'. Calmest when it's worst: 'Well, that's a nuisance.'",
      "wants": "Her lad home alive, the bridge held, and the King told the truth about what's on his road, which nobody at the palace has wanted to hear this month.",
      "fears": "Losing Wren, her sister's boy, on his first patrol. Dying for cushions, which she'd never live down.",
      "whyHere": "The one patrol on the King's Road this week: the Marshal sent her and Wren out, at the King's own wish, to meet the Assembly's cushions at the bridge and see them in. The goblins shot their horses an hour ago, and they've been pinned behind the hay wain since.",
      "knows": [
        "Marshal's orders, the King's wish: one patrol, the two of them, to see the Assembly's cushions in safe. Every other guard is lining the Assembly route.",
        "The goblins shot both horses at the bridge foot an hour ago. There's a war-boss across the river with a worg and a horn, waiting for them to break.",
        "The goblins have been bolder this month than in her twenty years in the guard: shooting oxen in daylight, always where they'll be seen, never taking anything worth the having.",
        "'After the Assembly' is the Marshal's answer to everything this month, and the palace keeps the road's troubles from the King.",
        "Garrow, the toll-warden: a pest, but he'll have written every arrow down."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "The arrow went deeper than she lets on: she can't lift her halberd above her shoulder and will go down if the fight goes long. She says nothing in front of Wren; it shows in how she keeps the halberd low."
      ],
      "attitude": "No time for strangers in a fight, and all the time in the world for one who's fought beside her. Grateful in deeds, not words: she'll put the player's name in the Marshal's ear and the King's.",
      "limits": "Never leaves Wren, never lets him be the hero if she can help it, never begs. If she goes down she's badly hurt and bleeding, not dead: whether she's up again soon is down to what the player does for her (a hand on the wound, Medicine, a spell or a potion).",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Barks orders, swears, ignores the arrow in her shoulder: steady as a wall.",
        "inconsistency": "She keeps her halberd low and won't lift her left arm.",
        "hooks": "The King's patrol and the King's wish; goblins who want to be seen; the war-boss across the river; a King who should be told what's on his road."
      },
      "statblock": "guard",
      "weapon": "halberd"
    },
    {
      "id": "tob",
      "name": "Guardsman Tobias Wren",
      "called": "the young guard",
      "aka": [
        "Tob",
        "Wren",
        "Guardsman Wren"
      ],
      "lore": "Cast/Guardsman Tobias Wren/public.md",
      "look": "Nineteen, long and thin as a rake in a steel cap too big for him and the King's green, holding his spear like a broom, with a fluff of moustache he's very proud of and a face that goes white, then red.",
      "voice": "Earnest, formal and breathless. Says 'In the King's name!' at everything, goblins included; calls the player 'sir' or 'madam' whatever they are; trips over his words when he's excited and goes very quiet when he's frightened. 'The King knows my name. He asked after my mother.'",
      "wants": "To do his duty, get his aunt (the corporal) home alive, and see the King thank whoever saved them: the King should know what's on his road, and Wren means to tell him.",
      "fears": "Being a coward. Aunt Maud dying because of him. The King being disappointed in him.",
      "whyHere": "The patrol's second man, on his first patrol outside the walls, sent with his aunt to bring the Assembly's cushions in safe.",
      "knows": [
        "The King asked for a patrol for the cushions himself: he takes his guests' comfort very seriously, down to the cushions. The Marshal grumbled, but sent two of them.",
        "The King told the Marshal, in Wren's hearing, that he wanted to be told of anything on the King's Road this week, himself, at once. Wren means to see he is.",
        "The King knows every guard's name, and how their mothers are.",
        "The city gates shut at the ninth bell for the Assembly; the King's green goes through any gate."
      ],
      "attitude": "Hero-worships the player from the first blow they strike for the patrol, can't do enough for them, and insists, politely and immovably, that they come and tell the King.",
      "limits": "Never leaves his aunt while she's down. Never lets the player not come to the King: he'll walk them there himself, by the hand if need be.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "'In the King's name!'; then, once it's over, shaking, grateful, and talking too much about the King.",
        "inconsistency": "Brave in the wrong places and frightened in the right ones.",
        "hooks": "The King asked to be told of anything on the road: the player must come and tell him."
      },
      "statblock": "guard",
      "weapon": "spear"
    },
    {
      "id": "garrow",
      "name": "Garrow",
      "called": "the toll-warden",
      "aka": [
        "Mister Garrow"
      ],
      "lore": "Cast/Garrow/public.md",
      "look": "A thin man of sixty in a bottle-green coat with brass buttons, a ledger the size of a door under his arm and a quill behind each ear, with a face like a disappointed heron. Seen only through a crack in his shutters until it's safe.",
      "voice": "Officious, nasal and furious, in the language of regulations ('The King's penny is levied upon every soul, beast and conveyance crossing the King's bridge, without exception'). Writes things down while he talks and reads them back. Lonely underneath it, and can't say so.",
      "wants": "The King's penny paid by everyone, his book kept straight and his toll house unburnt; and, though he'd never say it, to be thanked by someone, once.",
      "fears": "Goblins. The Treasury's auditors. Being blamed for the burnt toll pole.",
      "whyHere": "He keeps the King's toll bridge, as his father did; he barred himself in at the first arrow and has been writing it all down.",
      "knows": [
        "Every cart, beast and soul across the bridge is in his ledger, by date, with what they paid.",
        "Twelve palace grain wagons this month, wheat-ear canvas, laden, crossed his bridge inward to the city, and every one paid its penny. The city says palace wagons were 'lost to goblins'. Not on his bridge they weren't.",
        "The goblins have been on the road all month, but they never came at his bridge before today.",
        "Two palace guards and their horses this afternoon; the horses shot; the guards behind the hay wain."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "He has been keeping a tally of the goblins too, by number and by the hour, and the war-boss's name, which they shout: Grisk. He'd show it to anyone who thanked him."
      ],
      "attitude": "Hostile to anyone who hasn't paid. Won't open his door to anyone while there's a goblin in sight; after, he demands the King's penny from everyone, the dead goblins included. Thanked, he goes pink and doesn't know what to do with himself.",
      "limits": "Never opens his door while there's a goblin in sight. Never waives the toll on his own authority (the King's green can, and Wren does if asked). Never fights. Never lets go of his ledger.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "The King's penny, the regulations and the ledger, read aloud; outrage about the burnt toll pole.",
        "inconsistency": "He's been keeping the goblins' tally too, and he's frightened out of his wits.",
        "hooks": "His ledger, and the palace grain wagons that crossed his bridge laden all month: 'lost to goblins', not on his bridge."
      },
      "statblock": "commoner",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    },
    {
      "id": "rearguard",
      "name": "Wartle",
      "called": "the goblin with the drum",
      "look": "A squat goblin with a stolen palace helmet over one eye and a drum on a string, which he beats when he remembers to; with him, two more of the war-band round the toll-house corner, one with a scimitar and one with a bow.",
      "voice": "Shrieks 'Be seen!' and beats the drum; jeers at the soldiers in broken Common ('Shiny-hats! Shiny-hats hide!'); cornered, whines, bargains and offers pennies.",
      "wants": "To keep the shiny-hats pinned until the boss comes over, be seen doing it, and not die for it.",
      "fears": "The war-boss Grisk; fire; anyone coming at them from behind.",
      "whyHere": "Grisk's rearguard on the near bank, keeping the patrol pinned behind the hay wain while the war-boss waits across the river.",
      "knows": [
        "The boss Grisk is across the river with his worg and the rest, and will come over when the shiny-hats break.",
        "Longlegs pays them to be seen on the road, in pennies with a bird on; nobody knows Longlegs's name."
      ],
      "attitude": "Jeering while they're winning; the moment two of them are down or they're hurt, they run for the bridge and their boss.",
      "limits": "They don't know Longlegs's name. They don't fight to the death.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Drum, jeers and arrows at the hay wain; their backs to the road.",
        "inconsistency": "They're making a show of it, not a siege: shouting to be seen.",
        "hooks": "The war-boss across the river; Longlegs's pennies."
      },
      "group": "Wartle and two more of Grisk's war-band, the rearguard on the near bank",
      "statblock": "goblin-warrior",
      "weapon": "scimitar",
      "members": [
        {
