    {
      "id": "grisk",
      "name": "Grisk",
      "called": "the goblin war-boss on the worg",
      "look": "A big goblin, near as tall as a short man, in a palace breastplate hammered to fit and a crown of ox horns, with a scimitar, a horn on a string and a necklace of heron pennies, riding a worg the size of a pony: grey, scarred and foam-jawed, with yellow eyes.",
      "voice": "Roars in good Common, which he's proud of: 'Grisk is seen! All the longlegs see Grisk!'. Laughs at his own jokes, calls the player 'little longlegs'. Bargaining, he's very reasonable indeed.",
      "wants": "To be seen as no goblin has ever been seen, frighten the city on the eve of its Assembly and earn the longlegs' biggest purse; to keep his worg and his skin.",
      "fears": "Dying for pennies. Losing his worg. The longlegs, who frightens him, and whose name he doesn't know.",
      "whyHere": "Leading the burnt mill's war-band in the biggest show yet before the Assembly, as the longlegs paid for, and waiting across the river for the pinned patrol to break.",
      "knows": [
        "The longlegs pays in heron pennies for the band to be seen on the road: a tall one in a grey hood who smells of flowers, who comes to the burnt mill by night. Grisk doesn't know a name, and wishes he did (he'd ask for more).",
        "'Never take the wheat,' the longlegs said, and anyway, who would buy it?"
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "A week ago the longlegs paid Chief Gubbin extra for a job at a ford on the King's Road: a lady rider with a sealed letter, taken back to the burnt mill and kept there. Grisk wasn't on it, but he knows, and would trade it for his life."
      ],
      "attitude": "Mocking while he's winning, reasonable when he isn't; he'd sell anyone for his life, his own band first.",
      "limits": "Never fights to the death: once his worg is down or he's badly hurt he runs for the far bank, or, cornered, throws down his scimitar and bargains. He doesn't know the longlegs' name.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Horn, roar and swagger on the worg's back; then, beaten, very reasonable.",
        "inconsistency": "A war-boss who wants to be seen more than he wants to win.",
        "hooks": "The longlegs, the heron pennies, and the lady rider Chief Gubbin took at the ford."
      },
      "enters": "war-boss",
